Below is a concise, junior-engineer–friendly write up that points to the repo resources and explains what each piece contains and where to look for details.

Overview

Purpose: Terraform-based automation to provision a shared MySQL database (RDS / local) with per-developer schemas, IAM users, and credentials stored in Secrets Manager. Designed for local development (LocalStack + Docker) and AWS deployment.
Where to read full instructions

Root README.md — top-level project overview, architecture, quick-start commands for local and AWS, examples, cleanup, and a high-level project map. Start here for an end-to-end walkthrough.
HowTo/README.md — focused guide for the user-management script and CSV format (how to add users, required CSV fields, examples).
localsetup/README.md — local test details and how to run local-only checks; outputs are written to localsetup/run-local.log.
Key files and folders (what they contain and why you care)

envs/
envs/localstack.tfvars — configuration used for local runs (LocalStack). Edit this for local endpoints, DB credentials, or other env-specific values.
envs/prod.tfvars (or copy) — template for production values. Do not commit secrets.
main.tf, variables.tf — root Terraform module and variable definitions. Use these when adding or changing global infra behavior.
modules/
modules/rds/ — RDS / MySQL database configuration.
modules/iam/ — IAM users and policies for per-developer access.
modules/secrets/ — Secrets Manager resources used to store DB credentials.
modules/db_user_access/ — creation of DB users, schemas, and permission grants.
add-users.sh (root) and HowTo/add-users.sh — scripts to create users from a CSV into the target environment (the repo contains a user-management script; check both locations if unsure). Script validates CSV, updates tfvars (or user-specific var files), runs Terraform flows, shows created resources, and resets the CSV on success.
users.csv — template input for add-users.sh. Required header: FirstName,LastName,Email,Role. Valid roles: developer, admin, manager.
test-infrastructure.sh — smoke tests to verify the deployment (connectivity, resource presence).
localsetup/
README.md — step-by-step for running local tests.
run-local.log — captured output from local test runs; useful when debugging failures.
HowTo/README.md — shows expected CSV format, usage examples like ./add-users.sh users.csv local, and what the script outputs (IAM names, schema names, secret names).
Quick commands (what junior engineers will actually run)

Local environment start (LocalStack + MySQL container):
localstack start -d
docker run -d --name localstack-mysql -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=rootpassword -p 3306:3306 mysql:8.0
Configure AWS CLI for LocalStack (fake creds):
aws configure set aws_access_key_id test
aws configure set aws_secret_access_key test
aws configure set region eu-west-1
Deploy (local):
terraform init
terraform apply -var-file=envs/localstack.tfvars -auto-approve
Add users (example):
./add-users.sh users.csv local
(or) ./HowTo/add-users.sh users.csv local — see HowTo/README.md for exact script path used in your copy
Test:
./test-infrastructure.sh
Retrieve secrets:
LocalStack: awslocal secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id <secret-name>
AWS: aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id <secret-name>
Cleanup:
terraform destroy -var-file=envs/{env}.tfvars -auto-approve
docker stop localstack-mysql && docker rm localstack-mysql
localstack stop
What gets created (naming patterns)

IAM User: {env}-euwest1-{firstname}
Database Schema: {env}-euwest1-{firstname}_schema
Secret: {env}-euwest1-{firstname}-db-secret
DB user with role-specific permissions
Where to make changes (typical edits)

envs/{env}.tfvars — change sizes, endpoints, DB passwords (keep secrets out of VCS).
modules/* — change infra implementation or permissions.
add-users.sh — modify the user ingestion behavior, CSV handling, or tfvars injection logic.
test-infrastructure.sh / localsetup/* — alter or extend smoke tests for your needs.
Logs and debugging

Script runs produce stdout logs; some helper scripts write persistent logs. Check localsetup/run-local.log for local test output.
For secrets or API errors check LocalStack logs, Docker container logs (docker logs localstack-mysql), and Terraform plan/apply output.
Security and operational notes

Never commit production credentials/secrets to the repo.
Use least-privilege AWS credentials for deployment.
In CI, inject provider credentials via environment variables or a secrets manager.
Where to go next

Read root README.md for the full quick-start flow.
Follow HowTo/README.md to prepare users.csv and run the add-users script.
Use localsetup/README.md and run-local.log to validate a local deployment before touching AWS.

